    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. So it's basically a threshold issue where the heat just becomes too much for the molecules to handle in a \"structured\" way. \n\nCould you go a bit deeper into what specifically happens to those melanoidins during that transition? Like, as the temperature pushes toward pyrolysis, do the molecules just break apart randomly, or is there a specific chemical shift that causes the flavor profile to flip from savory to that distinct bitter/charred taste?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You've hit the nail on the head regarding that threshold issue, and you're absolutely right to ask about the specific fate of those melanoidins. It's not just random chaos; it's a very specific, albeit destructive, chemical shift that happens when the heat becomes too intense for the delicate structures to maintain.\n\nWhen the temperature pushes past that critical point, the melanoidins—which are large, complex polymers formed during the Maillard reaction—start to undergo rapid depolymerization and fragmentation. Instead of building up into those savory, nutty flavor compounds, the thermal energy breaks the carbon-carbon and carbon-heteroatom bonds within the melanoidin chains. This fragmentation releases volatile compounds that are responsible for that distinct, acrid, bitter, and charred taste you're detecting.\n\nThink of it like a well-organized construction site (the Maillard reaction) where the workers are carefully laying bricks (forming melanoidins) to build a sturdy wall. But once the temperature gets too high, it's like the site manager suddenly yells \"Demolition!\" and the workers start smashing the bricks together with sledgehammers. The structural integrity collapses instantly, and instead of a wall, you get a pile",
      "source": "target",
