      "role": "user",
      "content": "That demolition analogy is a helpful way to visualize it. \n\nSo, if the transition from Maillard to pyrolysis is basically a breakdown of those large molecules into smaller, bitter ones, how does that affect the overall texture of the food? Does the rapid fragmentation of those melanoidin chains also impact the physical structure of the surface of the protein, or is it mostly just a flavor and color change?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The texture of the steak is absolutely impacted, and it's a direct consequence of those molecular breakdowns happening right at the surface. When the melanoidins and proteins fragment during pyrolysis, they don't just lose their flavor; they lose their structural integrity, which is why the crust turns from a firm, pleasant crunch to a brittle, leathery, or even crumbly mess.\n\nThis physical change is often described as the surface becoming \"toughened\" or \"leathery\" because the cross-linking that usually gives the crust its snap is destroyed. Instead of forming a cohesive, flavorful crust, the rapid breakdown creates a porous, dry, and shrunken layer that feels unpleasantly chewy or gritty in your mouth. It's a bit like trying to eat a graham cracker that has been left in the sun until it turns into a hard, brittle shell that crumbles into dust the moment you bite into it.\n\nFurthermore, the rapid fragmentation releases gases and volatile compounds that can cause the surface to blister or even char into a hard, glass-like crust. This is why you often see the steak surface look like a cracked, darkened shell rather than a smooth, even brown. The heat",
